    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "makes sense. but looking at the actual legal theories being used, do you think the current regulatory framework is actually equipped to handle these digital markets, or is it kind of outdated and being forced to fit a square peg in a round hole?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Many legal experts believe the current framework, which often relies on 20th-century ideas of \"consumer welfare,\" is indeed strained by the unique nature of digital markets. These modern platforms exhibit \"winner-take-all\" network effects and data advantages that traditional anti-trust law was not designed to easily categorize.\n\nBecause the competitive advantage often comes from data and platform control rather than just price, many argue that more nuanced, potentially structural, or \"pro-competitive\" regulatory approaches are needed to effectively address these powerful and evolving digital gatekeepers.",
      "source": "target",
